now turning to an issue that is gaining  a lot of attention because of the war in  ukraine yes conflict is spotlighting the  struggles that many refugees face  putting down roots in a new country more  than 3.1 million refugees from around  the world now live in the united states  in both 2020 and 2021 about 12 000 new  refugees were admitted many of them are  children adriana diaz spoke with a group  of young refugees living in chicago who  are part of a community called reach  they talked about assimilating to the  united states and their memories of the  countries they used to call home  what i do remember is  sound of gunshots sound of  pain some of people stuck in the war for  40 years  that's all i remember from afghanistan  in burma there's military coup  so we can't go back to burma muhammad do  you remember what it was like in syria  or why you guys left we left because  there was a lot of war and it wasn't  pleasant lots of shooting to hear  children speak so plainly about war and  human suffering is not easy we came to  the u.s because my mom wanted like  a better education and just  life in general  my name is noer and my my intention for  today was to learn more about animals  but despite the odds these kids have  found hope and bonds with each other  through the similar stories of their  parents sacrifice how hard was it for  you guys to get used to life here in  chicago and making american friends and  and learning the culture when you step  out there's nobody really you can talk  to there's nobody you can share  your feelings to you feel very very  alone it was hard because we didn't  really know any english  so we couldn't communicate unfamiliar  language culture and unspoken societal  rules keep many refugees in their homes  but so much time indoors can deepen  feelings of isolation especially for  kids i was a little bit depressed  because i literally couldn't do anything  except stay at home but as i talked more  in school i started making more friends  and i started explaining my feelings  because what happens when you keep your  feelings bottled up you won't make  friends if you don't open your emotions  or talk to anyone  getting kids to open up and socialize is  the goal of reach a non-profit in  chicago that connects refugee youth  through activities i was approached by  several members of the local refugee  community they were worried because a  lot of their sons and daughters were  facing a lot of academic failure  social isolation  bullying i want us to think about a  couple of things that you want to try to  find shawna wills has worked with the  refugee community here in chicago for  more than 30 years and she knows the  pitfalls they're expected to  learn a new language adapt to new  culture understand a new school system  and then do that while also finding the  right peers they feel like they need to  fit in and so they're easy targets for  gang members we've seen a lot of refugee  young people who've lost their lives to  violence here so they're even more  vulnerable to gang violence and  recruitment very much so when they come  to this country for a better life yeah  you're going to make me cry because it  really we've lost a lot of kids  that's what keeps her on this now  lifelong mission to help these kids grow  into their own identities while becoming  familiar with their new country  the program exposes them to new  experiences designed to build confidence  and it also uses nature as therapy the  most important thing that happens is  that they start to feel a sense of  belonging  are you ready for this yeah already one  look at their faces and you see the  impact are you ready  what have you gained through reach a lot  of confidence being more social more  activities like more talking to people  reach makes me feel comfortable and  confident i was like shy and i wasn't  willing to go  out there and you know express myself so  does the us feel like home now yes  definitely  us feels like home because now everyone  is  equal people aren't  saying nice things they're not judging  anyone anymore i have a sense of  community  i don't feel like the odd one out  anymore i don't feel left out when we  first came here i had no confidence  in talking to anyone but now i feel more  confident in school and talking to  people  not being shy i kind of feel that the  united states is made of millions of  different stars and you know  by stars i mean different nations  different cultures and i think  one of the stars is my culture it is me  it is where i belong  for cbs mornings adriana diaz chicago  he said that very well the united states  is made up of a lot of different stars  you know when you're a kid you just want  to fit in that's right you just want  friends and you just want to fit in it's  good that they're now starting to feel  that you can only imagine what it's like  when you just move across the country  neighborhood and you go to a new school  that's hard  from another country and trying to fit  in you don't speak the language the  culture is different and sometimes  people aren't as welcoming as they  should be kids you know yeah um until  they learn how to be adults too right  and i think we all collectively can take  a little bit of what reach is doing in  chicago and apply it across the board i  mean you never know how isolated  somebody  is an adult a family of course kids um  but i think we all can look in the  mirror and say maybe we can do a little  bit more right for somebody who might  feel a little isolated i always say  kindness always works that's right all  levels it doesn't matter what you're  doing kindness always works that's right  gail i love that piece adriano yes well  done
 

